## Troubleshooting Notes

Phase 2 had several real troubleshooting moments — each one traced to a root cause rather than patched around.

| Problem | What Was Checked | Root Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kibana returned HTTP 503 "License information could not be obtained" right after setup | `journalctl -u kibana` | Missing Kibana saved-object encryption keys — the Debian package doesn't auto-generate them the way Elasticsearch auto-generates its security config. Kibana was stuck in a Fleet-setup retry loop; the 503 was a downstream symptom | Generated keys with `kibana-encryption-keys generate`, added the 3 lines to `kibana.yml`, restarted Kibana |
| Windows agent enrolled and showed `fleet: HEALTHY`, then flipped to Unhealthy — every data-shipping component failing with `dial tcp 10.0.2.15:9200 ... actively refused` | `elastic-agent status` on the Windows host | The Fleet Server install command's `--fleet-server-es=https://10.0.2.15:9200` auto-wrote a global default Fleet output in `kibana.yml`. That NAT-adapter IP was correct for Ubuntu-SIEM referring to itself, but Windows inherited the same output — and `10.0.2.15` on Windows resolves to *its own* NAT adapter, where nothing is listening | Fleet UI refused to edit the preconfigured output ("cannot be updated outside of kibana config file"), so the IP was corrected directly: `sed -i` on `xpack.fleet.outputs` in `kibana.yml`, then restart Kibana |
| After the output fix, most components went Healthy — but the agent's own self-monitoring components stayed stuck on the old IP | Kibana logs showed a "mismatched revisions" warning for the policy | Stale cached policy revision on the agent side | `Restart-Service "Elastic Agent"` on Windows forced a fresh policy pull (revision bumped 1 → 2), fully Healthy |
| Sysmon confirmed running locally (280+ events in Event Viewer), System integration confirmed shipping real Security/Application logs — but zero results querying for `Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon` | Discover, filtered by `event.provider` | Sysmon writes to its own separate event log channel (`Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational`), which the **System** integration does not collect | Added the separate **Windows** integration to the agent policy with "Sysmon Operational" enabled — no changes needed on the endpoint itself, the policy update deployed automatically |

**Lesson worth remembering:** any IP baked into a Fleet Server (or similar) CLI install command becomes a semi-permanent global default that *other* enrolled hosts inherit. Worth setting that to the host-only IP up front next time, even though the NAT IP is technically valid for the box installing it.

