## What Phase 2 Proved

- A working Elastic Stack SIEM pipeline can be stood up end-to-end on a single Ubuntu VM: ingest, storage, and visualization all live together and talk to each other correctly.
- Fleet-managed agents introduce a layer of indirection (policies, outputs, integrations) that behaves differently from a standalone Beat — output config in particular is global-by-default in a way that isn't obvious until it breaks a *second* host.
- Sysmon and Windows' native Security/Application/System logs are genuinely separate pipelines under the hood, even though they both eventually land in the same Elasticsearch cluster. "The agent is healthy" and "the specific telemetry I want is flowing" are two different questions.
- Suricata can be fully installed, tuned, and validated without ever touching Elastic — network IDS and endpoint SIEM are complementary but independently operable tools.

